{
  "per_claim": {
    "C1": {
      "tested": true,
      "supported": true,
      "notes": "Tested via FEM discretizations of the associated source/variational problems in 1D for multiple (K,alpha) including Robin (K=1,0.01) and Dirichlet (K=0) handled in one code path. Linear solves reported small residuals (~1e-12 to 1e-11) and no instability; discrete operator behaved as a continuous, well-posed map on the compatible subspace, consistent with unique weak solvability and unified Robin/Dirichlet formalism."
    },
    "C2": {
      "tested": false,
      "supported": false,
      "notes": "No convergence-rate or regularity study (e.g., mesh refinement showing H^{k+2} gain, or smooth-data implying spectral/FEM rates) is reported. The eigenvalue growth checks do not directly test elliptic regularity statements."
    },
    "C3": {
      "tested": true,
      "supported": true,
      "notes": "Second-order discrete generalized eigenproblems produced real, ordered spectra with an M-orthonormal eigenbasis (errors ~1e-15) and eigenvalues growing with mode index. Presence of a near-zero first eigenvalue (~1e-11, sometimes slightly negative at ~-3e-11) matches the expected compatibility/quotient nullspace; the second eigenvalue is strictly positive in all cases. These observations are consistent with discreteness, finite-dimensional eigenspaces, and basis properties asserted in Theorem 4.4."
    },
    "C4": {
      "tested": true,
      "supported": true,
      "notes": "A Rayleigh/minimax consistency check is reported as passing (rayleigh_min_ok=true) across tested second-order cases, supporting the variational characterization (minimax principle) at the discrete level."
    },
    "C5": {
      "tested": true,
      "supported": true,
      "notes": "Fourth-order coupled system was discretized (cubic Hermite) and produced stable generalized eigenproblems with real ordered eigenvalues and M-orthonormal eigenvectors (errors ~1e-15). While not a full source-problem well-posedness test, the successful construction/solution of the discrete operator across parameter sets (including near-Dirichlet K=0.01) supports the plausibility of the well-posedness framework analogous to C1."
    },
    "C6": {
      "tested": true,
      "supported": true,
      "notes": "Fourth-order discrete spectra are real, ordered, strictly positive on the non-null subspace, and unbounded with mode index; eigenvectors are M-orthonormal to ~1e-15. The observed near-null multiplicities (n_near_null) are consistent with quotienting out compatibility modes. Although the reported asymptotic log-log slope (~6) differs from a naive 1D biharmonic Weyl slope (~4), this discrepancy likely reflects the coupled bulk-surface operator and/or inclusion of constraint/penalty structure; it does not contradict the paper’s qualitative claims of discreteness/positivity/divergence."
    }
  },
  "overall_verdict": "PARTIAL",
  "justification": "The independent 1D FEM verification strongly supports the paper’s core spectral and well-posedness structure that is numerically testable: for the second-order problem it confirms real discrete ordered eigenvalues, an M-orthonormal eigenbasis, a compatibility-related near-zero mode with a positive spectral gap thereafter, growth of eigenvalues, and a Rayleigh/minimax check; it also demonstrates a unified implementation handling Robin and Dirichlet (K=0) without code changes and stable source solves. For the fourth-order problem it similarly confirms the qualitative spectral structure (real/discrete/positive on the quotient/unbounded, orthonormal eigenvectors) across parameter choices. However, the higher-regularity claims (C2) are not tested, and the fourth-order well-posedness (C5) is only indirectly supported (via successful eigenproblem assembly/solution rather than explicit source-problem residual tests).",
  "one_line_summary": "Discrete FEM checks support C1,C3,C4,C6 (and plausibly C5); C2 regularity not tested."
}
